WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.200
How much gas do you need for the dive?

00:03.051 --> 00:08.551
You plan a dive to twenty metres for thirty-five minutes, with a tank filled to two hundred bar.

00:08.551 --> 00:10.851
Is that fill enough for the dive and a reserve?

00:11.690 --> 00:13.840
Let's calculate the answer before you dive.

00:14.940 --> 00:20.600
The gas a dive needs is easy to remember: a cat, C times A times T.

00:20.760 --> 00:24.100
C is your consumption, the surface consumption rate, SCR.

00:24.100 --> 00:26.900
It is the litres you breathe per minute at the surface.

00:26.900 --> 00:29.420
A is the average pressure, in atmospheres.

00:29.540 --> 00:31.550
T is the time, in minutes.

00:34.150 --> 00:41.790
For example, in our dive the average depth is twenty metres, which is also the deepest point, and the dive lasts thirty-five minutes.

00:41.810 --> 00:47.680
The tank holds eleven point one litres, and your SCR is fifteen litres per minute.

00:49.430 --> 00:52.950
Step one: let's calculate the average pressure, A.

00:52.950 --> 00:56.960
Twenty metres divided by ten, plus one, is three atmospheres.

00:58.210 --> 01:03.590
Step two: let's calculate the gas you breathe per minute at depth, C times A.

01:03.590 --> 01:07.920
Fifteen litres per minute times three atmospheres is forty-five litres per minute.

01:10.220 --> 01:15.060
Step three: let's calculate the gas for the whole dive, times T.

01:15.180 --> 01:20.510
Forty-five litres per minute times thirty-five minutes is one thousand five hundred and seventy-five litres.

01:21.960 --> 01:26.880
Step four: let's calculate the same gas in bar, because your gauge shows bar.

01:26.920 --> 01:33.650
One thousand five hundred and seventy-five litres divided by the tank's volume, eleven point one litres, is one hundred and forty-two bar.

01:35.950 --> 01:40.190
Now let's calculate the total: the gas for the dive plus minimum gas.

01:40.190 --> 01:46.430
Minimum gas is the gas two divers need to ascend from the deepest point, and for twenty metres it is sixty bar.

01:46.510 --> 01:51.110
One hundred and forty-two bar plus sixty bar is two hundred and two bar.

01:51.928 --> 01:54.008
That is more than the two hundred bar fill.

01:56.208 --> 02:00.928
The rule: your tank pressure must cover the gas for the dive plus minimum gas.

02:01.714 --> 02:06.214
This fill is not enough, so reduce the dive time, or take a bigger tank.

02:07.025 --> 02:10.775
How to calculate minimum gas is in another video on this channel.

02:13.375 --> 02:18.455
Turn Pressure calculates both for your dive: the gas, and the minimum gas.

02:18.635 --> 02:20.035
Free on the App Store.

02:20.819 --> 02:22.229
diver dot today.
